<Base
  title="Disclaimer"
  description="General information about investing, published impersonally and on a schedule. Not advice, not a recommendation to buy or sell anything, and never an answer to a reader's question about their own portfolio."
  asOf={null}
>
  <Hero
    title="Disclaimer"
    lede="Everything here is general information about how investing works. None of it is advice, and none of it takes any account of your situation."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="general">It is general, and it is impersonal</h2>

    <p>The results here were measured for a US federal individual investor at particular tax brackets, with
    particular account types, over particular stretches of history. You are not that person. Nothing here knows
    your income, your tax bracket, your state, your debts, when you plan to stop working, or how you behaved the
    last time your account fell by a third. Every one of those changes the answer.</p>

    <p>Naming a fund is a measurement, never a suggestion that you own it. No offer and no solicitation of any
    kind is being made.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="impersonal">I will never look at your portfolio</h2>

    <p>Not by email, not in an issue, not privately, and not with the names changed. A writer who reads your
    holdings and tells you what to do with them is giving personal investment advice, whatever the page says at
    the bottom, and a publisher stays outside the Investment Advisers Act only as long as the writing stays
    entirely impersonal.</p>

    <p>If you want somebody to look at your own portfolio and tell you what to do, hire a fee-only fiduciary
    adviser who is paid by you and by nobody else. Questions about the method, an error in a number, or a source
    that does not say what a page claims are welcome. The answer will be a correction, not a recommendation.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="cadence">It is published on a schedule, never because the market moved</h2>

    <p>Pages are revised when new evidence lands or on a date written into the page in advance. Nothing here will
    ever be published because the market moved. That is partly a legal condition and partly a finding: a rule
    reviewed on price cannot be told apart from a rule abandoned in a fall.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="risk">Investing can lose you everything</h2>

    <p>All investing carries risk, including the loss of everything you put in. Past performance tells you what
    happened, not what will happen. Every portfolio here has spent long stretches behind a plain index fund, and
    the site says by how much: cheap US companies ran <Figure id="value-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> the
    US market across nearly eighteen years and never caught up, and international stocks ran
    <Figure id="intl-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> US stocks over a similar stretch. Both figures are
    measured history, not simulations.</p>

    <h3 id="backtests">Backtested results are hypothetical</h3>

    <p>A backtest is what a rule would have produced if somebody had run it, on data chosen after the fact, by
    someone who already knows how the story ends. Nobody earned those returns, no fee was paid, and nobody sat
    through the fall in the middle without knowing how it ended. Read every backtested number here down for
    that, on top of the range printed beside it.</p>

    <h3 id="kelly">Position sizing assumes numbers nobody has</h3>

    <p>This site is named after the Kelly criterion, which works out the bet size that grows your money fastest
    when you know the odds. You do not know the odds, nobody does, and the arithmetic does not fail gently when
    you are wrong. Overestimate your advantage by a factor of two and the formula that maximised growth now
    destroys it. Every probability printed here is a best case, and the real chance is lower by an amount nobody
    can state.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="scope">Tax, jurisdiction and accuracy</h2>

    <p>The tax arithmetic assumes a US federal individual investor and ignores every state and local tax. It is
    not tax advice, and a result that holds in a US taxable account is sometimes exactly backwards elsewhere.
    Talk to somebody licensed before acting on it.</p>

    <p>No warranty is given on any of this: not on the accuracy of a number, not on a source still saying what
    it said when it was read, and not on the site being available. I accept no liability for any loss arising
    from anything on this site, to the fullest extent the law allows.</p>

    <h3 id="uk">United Kingdom</h3>

    <p>This site is not directed at retail consumers in the United Kingdom, and nothing on it is a financial
    promotion under section 21 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000. I am not authorised or regulated
    by the Financial Conduct Authority.</p>

    <h3 id="not-legal-advice">This page is not legal advice either</h3>

    <p>The same non-lawyer wrote it. It describes the conditions this site intends to keep, not a legal opinion
    that it has kept them. <a href="/about/">The about page</a> and
    <a href="/disclosures/">the disclosures page</a> are the context.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
